The PR is a security fix. The base code chained `.filter(teams__members=user).filter(teams__access=...)` on the `Project` queryset, which Django resolves as two independent joins (an OR across the related teams), so it returned a project as long as the user was a member of *any* team and the project belonged to *any* team with the given access — granting access to teams the user wasn't part of. The PR consolidates both conditions into a single `Q` object (`Q(teams__members=user) & Q(teams__access=...)`), which forces both conditions to hold against the *same* team row, correctly restricting access.

This removes an over-permissive result set (a fix for CWE-863/missing-authorization exposure), and no caller depends on the old over-broad behavior. I find no newly introduced authorization flaw in this section.

